# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. THE C LATTICE AND THE REFITTED ANCHORS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# corpus       TRAIN benchmarks/zensim_loop_manifest_2026-08-06.txt (12 sources)
#              VAL   benchmarks/zensim_lattice_manifest_val_2026-08-06.txt (12)
#              Disjoint by SOURCE (the fitter re-asserts overlap == 0).
#              These are the same splits the B constants were fitted on, so
#              the two fits are comparable corpus-for-corpus.
# sizes        long edges 64 and 256. **NO 1024 TIER** — a deliberate budget
#              truncation, not an oversight. The committed B fit covers
#              {64, 256, 1024}, so the headline aggregates are NOT directly
#              comparable to it; the size-matched B refit below exists
#              precisely so the comparison is like-for-like.
# cells        24 per split (12 sources x 2 sizes), 0 dropped, 0 malformed
#              rows. 5,575 measured encodes TRAIN + 5,695 VAL = 11,270.
# sweep        every quantizer index the coarse probe brackets. Under C the
#              probe never found a qi scoring >= 96 or <= 12 on most cells,
#              so nearly every cell swept the FULL qi 8..248 — i.e. the C
#              lattice here is complete, not banded. That is itself a
#              finding: C's reachable dial on this corpus is narrower than
#              B's at both ends (median cell max ~93, median cell min ~14).
# fitter       scripts/hyperparam/fit_zensim_two_shot.py (unchanged), which
#              replays each placement rule offline against the measured
#              per-quantizer curves and splits the error into LATTICE (how
#              far the nearest achievable score is) and PREDICTION.
#
# REFITTED CONSTANT (now in src/zensim_c.rs as ANCHOR_QUANTIZER_C):
#   235.500, 228.000, 222.500, 213.000, 201.500, 192.000, 179.000, 162.000,
#   155.000, 148.500, 127.500, 116.000,  98.500,  70.000,  33.500
# Shipped B table for contrast (src/two_pass_zensim.rs ANCHOR_QUANTIZER):
#   205.622, 199.214, 192.630, 186.827, 180.797, 172.038, 166.717, 159.257,
#   149.813, 136.027, 126.477, 115.037,  92.745,  66.220,  26.318
# The two differ by ~30 quantizer steps at the coarse end and converge near
# the fine end. Reusing B's table for C is a measurable error, not a nuance:
# VAL median |err| 2.616 (B knots) vs 1.579 (C knots), mean -0.576 paired,
# better on 54.5% of 684 pairs, sign-test p = 0.0002.
#
# NOT SHIPPED: the pass-2 gain sweep picked g = 0.85 on TRAIN (median 1.170
# vs 1.449 at g = 1.0) but the same arm is NOT distinguishable on VAL
# (mean -0.173, p = 0.0877). Same outcome the B fit reached; the principled
# g = 1 pure translate stays.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. TWO-SHOT ERROR DECOMPOSITION: C vs B, SIZE-MATCHED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both columns are the same fitter, same corpus, same rule, same {64,256}
# size mix, VAL split only. The B column was produced by re-running the
# fitter on the committed 2026-08-06 B lattice filtered to sizes 64 and 256
# (awk on the decompressed TSV) — that is what makes it comparable; the
# numbers in src/two_pass_zensim.rs's module docs include a 1024 tier and
# are therefore NOT the right baseline for this table.
#
#   VAL, policy=nearest, rule = qi_translate on that profile's REFIT knots
#
#   metric                       B (n=666)     C (n=684)
#   ORACLE / LATTICE median         0.156         0.126
#   pass 1 alone (open loop)        2.891         4.537
#   two-shot median |err|           1.032         1.579
#   two-shot p90                    4.248         8.027
#   two-shot p99                   10.461        16.745
#   PREDICTION median               0.783         1.366
#   LATTICE share of the error      15.2%          8.0%
#   PREDICTION share                84.8%         92.0%
#
# READING IT HONESTLY. C's achievable-score lattice is FINER than B's
# (0.126 vs 0.156 median distance to the nearest reachable score), so the
# ceiling on precision is slightly better. But C is HARDER TO PLACE: the
# open-loop pass-1 error is +57% and the two-shot placement error +75%, and
# the net two-shot precision is worse — 1.032 -> 1.579 median, with a much
# heavier tail (p90 4.25 -> 8.03). The B finding that the prediction term
# dominates is not just preserved under C, it is stronger: 92% vs 85%.
#
# Where C's extra error lives (VAL, C, by size and target band):
#   size=64   median 3.637   p90 11.851     size=256  median 0.791  p90 3.778
#   t[20,40)  2.710     t[40,60) 1.566     t[60,80) 1.724   t[80,91) 0.510
# So it is concentrated at TINY sizes and LOW targets. C's adjacent-quantizer
# gap in the [10,30) score band has median 1.985 and p90 9.397 — the dial is
# very coarse down there, which both inflates placement error and makes the
# anchor's low-score knots the least trustworthy part of the refit.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. SPATIAL (steering) ARM UNDER C — MEASURED, AND IT DOES NOT STEER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# zensim_c_steer_ab_2026-08-07.tsv. Matched-QUANTIZER A/B: encode plain,
# build the per-SB map from C's OWN attribution density via
# zensim_c::sb_q_scale_from_attribution, re-encode at the SAME quantizer
# with the map, rescore with C. 4 held-out sources x long edges {256, 1024}
# x quantizers {60, 100, 140} x strengths {0.5, 1.0} = 48 cells. The map was
# live: zenravif::FRAME_HINTS_LIVE == true, per-SB scales spanned the full
# clamp domain (min 0.400, max 2.500 at strength 1.0), and the FD gradient
# was non-degenerate on every cell (654-666 of 944 columns non-zero — the
# rest are the bake's pruned dead columns, exactly as expected).
#
#   strength 0.5   d_bytes median +2.67%  (-8.40 .. +26.60)
#                  d_C     median +0.051  (-2.014 .. +2.310)  13/24 improved
#   strength 1.0   d_bytes median +4.46%  (-16.94 .. +77.71)
#                  d_C     median -1.299  (-5.994 .. +3.537)   6/24 improved
#
# THE MAP IS NOT RATE-NEUTRAL, which is the first thing that breaks. The B
# derivation's rate-neutrality argument rests on the scales having geometric
# mean 1; the attribution policy normalizes on the ARITHMETIC mean of the
# positive part, so it does not inherit that property — and empirically the
# same quantizer with the map on moves the file by -17% to +78%.
#
# SO READ THE TWO DELTAS TOGETHER, NEVER d_C ALONE:
#   cells that gained score WITHOUT spending bytes:            0 of 48
#   near-rate-matched cells (|d_bytes| <= 2.5%):               n = 13
#       d_C median -0.646, improved 2/13, best +0.42, worst -4.45
#
# VERDICT: at matched rate, C's attribution map steered through this policy
# does not help. Zero cells out of 48 bought score for free. The apparent
# +0.05 median at strength 0.5 is bytes, not steering. The default stays 0.0
# (off) and there is now a measurement behind that default rather than an
# inherited assumption.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT CONVICT. It convicts THIS POLICY (arithmetic-
# mean ratio, clamp [0.4, 2.5], power -strength) on THIS channel (per-SB
# delta-q). It does not convict the map: the same directional result has now
# been reached three times through per-SB delta-q — B's diffmap here, the
# attribution map here, and zensim's own probe below — which points at the
# channel. Two known channel-level confounds, both shared by any map source:
# sb_q_scale hints make zenrav1e disable its own segmentation (so the hinted
# arm loses the encoder's k-means ALT_Q adaptation), and per-SB delta_q
# costs syntax bits and taxes spatial prediction. zensim's registered next
# lever is the lambda side instead (zenrav1e's per-16x16 ssim_rdmult, no
# syntax cost, composes with segmentation) — untried here.
#
# Caveats: n=4 sources, matched-quantizer not matched-rate (no per-arm rate
# ladder), one policy family, two strengths, speed 6 only.
#
# The corroborating external data point, from zensim's own AVIF probe
# (benchmarks/avif_sb_probe_2026-08-06.meta in imazen/zensim, campaign
# appendix Y.R3): the geomean-ratio policy applied to the ATTRIBUTION map
# and fed to zenrav1e's per-SB delta-q lost at matched rate on 9/9 cells
# (Delta ssim2 -0.51..-4.07). Two named confounds: sb_q_scale hints disable
# zenrav1e's own segmentation, and delta_q syntax costs bits. n=3 images,
# one policy, 576^2 only. It is a reason to keep the arm off by default, not
# a verdict on the map.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. 2x2 / 4x4 SUBSAMPLED POOLING — MEASURED, NOT WORTH IT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# zensim_pool_subsample_2026-08-07.tsv. 4 held-out sources x long edges
# {256, 1024}, qi 80, 7 reps, arms INTERLEAVED within each rep (never
# all-A-then-all-B) so thermal drift cannot fake a delta; medians reported.
# A 4096 tier was requested and is absent for a corpus reason: clic2025 tops
# out at a 2048 long edge and upscaling is banned, so the harness skips it.
#
# Medians over the 4 sources:
#
#   long edge 256                 1024
#   B score-only        2.172 ms       33.736 ms
#   B score+diffmap     2.266 ms       35.041 ms   (map marginal 0.095 / 1.305)
#   pool full-res       0.259 ms        4.378 ms
#   pool 2x2            0.065 ms        1.057 ms   (3.97x / 4.14x faster)
#   pool 4x4            0.017 ms        0.268 ms   (15.6x / 16.4x faster)
#   C score-only        3.902 ms       75.324 ms   (1.80x / 2.23x the B score)
#   C score+attribution 112.4 ms       381.6 ms    (28.8x / 5.1x the C score)
#   C sb_means (SAT)    0.0005 ms       0.0013 ms
#
# WHICH HALF COSTS. For B the pooling is NOT negligible against the metric
# call — 4.38 ms is 12.5% of the whole score+map call at 1024, and 3.4x the
# diffmap's own marginal production cost (1.3 ms). Subsampling attacks the
# larger of the two halves, which is the right half.
#
# BUT AGAINST AN ENCODE IT IS NOISE. Same box, same config, single-threaded
# encode at 1024 / speed 6 / q77: 1,666 / 5,082 / 3,779 / 5,263 ms on these
# four sources. Pooling full-res is ~0.1% of one encode; 2x2 saves ~3.3 ms,
# i.e. ~0.08%. A two-shot spends two encodes. THE ANSWER IS NO: subsampling
# saves a tenth of a percent of a two-shot and is not worth the complexity.
#
# NATIVE REDUCED RESOLUTION IS NOT AVAILABLE. zensim's DiffmapOptions has no
# resolution or stride knob (weighting / masking_strength / sqrt /
# include_edge_mse / include_hf / guided_coarse_redistribution), and
# AttributionResult has none either. So the production half cannot be asked
# for a cheaper map; post-hoc subsampling of the pooling is the only lever,
# and it is the smaller prize by ~40x against an encode.
#
# FOR C THE QUESTION IS MOOT ON THE POOLING SIDE. AttributionResult carries a
# summed-area table, so per-superblock reduction is O(1) per block:
# sb_means costs 1.3 MICROseconds at 1024. Every cost is in producing the
# map (5.1x the C score at 1024, 28.8x at 256 — the FD gradient's 2 forwards
# per live bake column is a fixed cost that does not shrink with the image).
# There is nothing for subsampling to save.
#
# ACCURACY, since speed alone is not the answer. Per-superblock |delta| in
# the resulting q_scale map (clamped domain [0.4, 2.5]), full-res vs
# subsampled, over the same 8 cells:
#   2x2   median-of-medians 0.0071 (256) / 0.0061 (1024);  worst cell max 0.526
#   4x4   median-of-medians 0.0286 (256) / 0.0200 (1024);  worst cell max 1.475
# So 2x2 is near-identical for the typical superblock but can move a single
# block by half the useful range, and 4x4 by more than the whole of it. That
# is what a 12-norm pool does: it is nearly a max, and a max over 1 pixel in
# 4 misses spikes. Even if the time mattered, 4x4 would be a trade, not free.
#
